Z||000062||Slammed Fiero||02-24-2001||11:17 AM||||Read the post , He says he has a Type R motor with minor Mods. It's not 230Hp @ the wheels , Thats more than the S2000 motor , it's 230 at the motor.<P><BR>""and he has an intake/header and exhaust. ""<P><BR>Even Still when guys running huge Boost numbers are high 11's in cars that weigh marginally more than a CRX if not the same,  a 230hp CRX running 11's doesn't compute.<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000063||Black88GT||02-24-2001||03:55 PM||||Hmm I never said he had minor mods to the motor, I said he tweaked the motor to make 230 Hp.  He estimated that because his friend did a similar swap on a integra and it dynoed 230 Hp to the wheels.  Also, you say "fully gutted interior" well I clearly stated the interior is NOT fully gutted because it's not a trailor queen.  No local hang out around me has streetable muscle cars running 10s.  More like 12s - 13s.  There is one tubbed 68 Camaro that runs 10s with NOS and slicks, but not even close to street legal.  I didnt talk long enough to this CRX kid to get more details, it was maybe a 5 min. conversation.  Unless this guy has another CRX that runs 10s this wasn't a B.S. timeslip because I have been to the track where he went.  I'm not trying to flame either or get into some Import vs. Domestic argument, but too many domestic owners (esp. Fiero owners) tend to laugh and make fun of "ricers" when 9 out of 10 times that "ricer" would dust their Fiero/domestic.  I'm not gonna call any import tuner around here a ricer unless I can back it up.  It's easy to sit behind a computer and say "hahaha some ricer tried to race me today" or whatever, but when your lined up against what appears to be a modded import, we'll see who is the ricer.  The import scene has exploded and running 11s or 12s is "slow" with the serious racers.  Run 11s or 12s in a Fiero and your walking on water...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">               <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">               <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000064||hugh||02-24-2001||04:14 PM||||Sleeper,12.55@132mph doesn't compute unless he started in high gear and has about 700 hp for the weight of the car(probably around 3000 lbs).Also reaction time doesn't have anything to do with elapsed time.||||reg||1
Z||000065||Sleeper||02-24-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>Sleeper,12.55@132mph doesn't compute unless he started in high gear and has about 700 hp for the weight of the car(probably around 3000 lbs).Also reaction time doesn't have anything to do with elapsed time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ok, i'm sure he still has the time slip. if he would have gotten a better reaction time he probably would have gotten a better et. i think he has 700 hp or so at the crank but i know for sure not at the wheels. he has a 4 grand stall, would that be considered "high gear"?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000066||hugh||02-25-2001||07:51 AM||||Sleeper,1/4 mile speed is a result of horsepower,elapsed time is a determined by a number of things.Chassis settup,which affects traction on the bottom end and handling on the top end,being the most important.Driver ability and experience.A high stall torque converter lets the engine get into the best rpm range for launch and acceleration.the rpm range will vary with engine size,and many other criteria.<BR>Reaction time has nothing to do with elapsed time.It is only a measure of the time from when the green lightcomes on to when your vehicle leaves the starting line beams.Your reaction time could be 2 minutes and it wouldn't affect your elapsed time.  ||||reg||1
